harness: jxl-encoder/examples/rd_monotonicity_gate.rs
command: --images 6 --size 512 --efforts 5,8 (22-point distance ladder)
corpus: the EXACT set nightly uses -- scripts/hunt/fetch_imazen26_gate_files.sh,
  5 sha256-pinned images from imazen-26-unprocessed (NOAA document, manuscript
  scan, aliased plot, web screenshot, product shot)
cells: 220
oracles: SSIMULACRA2 (fast-ssim2 PINNED 0.7.1) + butteraugli; a hard violation
  needs BOTH to agree the ordering inverted
host: Mac aarch64

RESULT: IQA monotonicity CLEAN -- 0 corroborated violations.
  13 advisory byte inversions (2 at filter boundaries).
  allowlist: 0 of 3 entries name an image in this corpus.

WHY THAT LAST LINE MATTERS: the three known violations live on local `png-v3`
renders (5058, 2400_textures), which are NOT in the CI gate set -- different
pixels, different filenames. So nightly's "0 known" is expected and correct, and
its "clean" verdict is a genuine result ON THIS CORPUS rather than the allowlist
hiding anything. Verified by running the identical corpus locally.
